WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
Four drugs act on one transmitter, with opposite effects.

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.320
What decides which way each one pushes?

3
00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:16.643
In 1921, Otto Loewi reported slowing a frog heart by stimulating its vagus nerve.

4
00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:21.637
He moved the fluid around it onto a second heart, and that heart slowed too.

5
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:24.217
Its own nerve was untouched.

6
00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:26.917
The first nerve had released a chemical.

7
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:32.527
If the message is a chemical, a drug can imitate it, block it, or make it last.

8
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:35.747
Follow one molecule across a synapse.

9
00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:37.847
Three things happen to it.

10
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.650
First, it is released from the terminal, mostly when a spike arrives.

11
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.530
Second, it is read by a receptor, a protein on the target cell.

12
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:52.003
Some receptors are ion channels that open within milliseconds.

13
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:55.677
Others are G-protein-coupled receptors.

14
00:00:56.697 --> 00:01:00.837
They act through messengers inside the cell, over tens of milliseconds to minutes.

15
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:06.927
Third, it is removed, by an enzyme or by a transporter that pulls it back into a

16
00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:07.927
cell.

17
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.600
Source, readers, off switch.

18
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.420
Every transmitter fills these three slots.

19
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:16.803
Think of a key, a lock, and a cleaner that sweeps keys away.

20
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:20.817
A copy of the key that turns the lock is an agonist.

21
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.380
A key that fits but will not turn is a competitive antagonist.

22
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.440
Oil in the lock, so the real key turns more easily, is a positive allosteric

23
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.440
modulator.

24
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.663
Stopping the cleaner is a reuptake blocker or an enzyme inhibitor.

25
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:41.183
GABA is the brain's main fast inhibitory transmitter.

26
00:01:42.163 --> 00:01:44.183
Here are four drugs that act on its system.

27
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.633
Baclofen activates GABA-B receptors.

28
00:01:48.673 --> 00:01:52.113
Bicuculline sits in the GABA site of GABA-A, without opening it.

29
00:01:53.017 --> 00:01:57.357
Diazepam binds a different site on GABA-A and strengthens GABA's effect.

30
00:01:58.357 --> 00:02:00.757
Tiagabine blocks GAT1, a GABA transporter.

31
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.620
Picture the receiving cell at a mature synapse, where GABA inhibits.

32
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.120
Which drugs make inhibition stronger, and which weaker?

33
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:18.263
Baclofen, diazepam and tiagabine make it stronger.

34
00:02:19.343 --> 00:02:22.403
Bicuculline weakens it, and in animals it causes seizures.

35
00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:27.743
On terminals, baclofen can also cut GABA release, so circuits can differ.

36
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.887
Same transmitter, opposite effects.

37
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:35.067
What decides is which step a drug changes, and in which direction.

38
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.570
The idea: classify a drug by the main step it changes.

39
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.830
Release, reading, or removal.

40
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.800
How much it changes that step depends on how much of its target it fills.

41
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.590
What that does to a disease is a separate question.

42
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.680
The takeaway: find the step a drug changes, and the direction it pushes.
